// The section "Components on the server" of the English guide; guide.rk lists the sections.

import { Example } from "../../example.rk"

export comp ServerComponents() {
    return <>
        <p>
            The same markup and components write a server. A package with
            {" "}<code>{'"rook": { "markup": "html" }'}</code> in its <code>package.json</code> gets
            markup that is a string of HTML, of type <code>Html</code>, and components that answer
            requests. The root takes the <code>Request</code> of the web standard, and
            {" "}<code>serve(App)</code> of <code>rooklang/server</code> runs it on
            {" "}<code>node:http</code>; <code>handler(App)</code> is a function from
            {" "}<code>Request</code> to <code>Response</code> for Bun, Deno and Workers. This is
            experimental and may change before 1.0.
        </p>
        <Example name="server-components" />
        <ul>
            <li>
                Text and attributes are <b>escaped</b>; an <code>Html</code> goes in as it is, and
                {" "}<code>html(text)</code> marks a string you trust as HTML. Attributes are
                checked as in the browser, and <code>{"<html>"}</code> gets a doctype. What only a
                page has is an error: <code>onClick</code>, <code>bind:</code>, <code>ref</code>,
                {" "}<code>state</code>, <code>mount</code>.
            </li>
            <li>
                A component is an <b>async</b> function: its body may <code>await</code>, and a tag
                is its result, waited for. Components side by side in markup render at the same
                time.
            </li>
            <li>
                A component returns <b>any value</b>; its type is inferred, or written after the
                properties: <code>comp PostList() Post[]</code>. The answer is made from the value
                of the root: a <code>Response</code> as it is, <code>Html</code> as
                {" "}<code>text/html</code>, a string as text, <code>null</code> as 404, the rest as
                JSON. <code>reply(value, {"{ status, headers }"})</code> gives a status and
                headers.
            </li>
            <li>
                A <code>Response</code> among the children of markup <b>goes up</b>: the render
                stops, and it is the answer to the request. The other components still end, so
                their <code>defer</code> runs.
            </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            Decorators are the middleware. Their bodies may wait too, and an early
            {" "}<code>return</code> is a <b>guard</b>: it answers instead of the component. A
            decorator applied to a component above is the <b>context</b> of the components below:
            they name it with <code>needs</code> and read its public members.
        </p>
        <Example name="server-context" />
        <ul>
            <li>
                <code>needs session</code> is satisfied by <code>@session</code> on the component
                itself or on any component above it in the tree of the request; the nearest one
                is taken. Where a component is used by its name, the compiler checks that one is
                there.
            </li>
            <li>
                <code>dec admin() needs session</code> takes <code>@session</code> from above, so
                {" "}<code>@admin</code> works on <code>Dashboard</code>, which does not apply
                {" "}<code>@session</code> itself.
            </li>
            <li>
                The children are <code>Content{"<T>"}</code>, a function that renders them when
                called: <code>Logger</code> decides when they run, and gives what they give. Its
                {" "}<code>T</code> is inferred from the children of the tag, here the
                {" "}<code>Html | Response</code> of <code>Dashboard</code> with its guard.
            </li>
            <li>
                A wrapper, <code>{"return (page Html) => <main>{page}</main>"}</code>, gets what the
                component gives, of any type, as in the browser. The server of this site uses one
                to log the status of every answer.
            </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            <code>route("/orders/:id", Order)</code> ties a path to a page: the parameters of the
            path are its properties, and the compiler checks one against the other. The router is
            a loop over the table.
        </p>
        <Example name="server-routes" />
        <ul>
            <li>
                The path is a string literal. Each <code>:name</code> is a property of the page
                that takes a string, and each property the page needs is in the path, so a typo in
                either is an error. <code>match(path)</code> gives the page with them, or
                {" "}<code>null</code>; <code>Route{"<T>"}</code> has the <code>T</code> the page
                gives.
            </li>
            <li>
                A wrapper that takes <code>Content{"<T>"}</code> gets the <b>body</b> of the
                component instead of what it gave, and runs it itself: inside
                {" "}<code>try</code>, it is an error boundary. A <code>Response</code> from below
                still goes up through <code>try</code>.
            </li>
        </ul>
    </>
}
